                <h2>Why Your Multi-Account Management Is Always Chaotic</h2>
<p>When a team manages dozens of accounts simultaneously, the most common problems are: budget allocation based on intuition, duplicate ad creative delivery, and uneven account performance with no follow-up.</p>
<p>The essence of account tiering management is to ensure each account receives appropriate resource investment while reducing communication costs in team collaboration.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Establish Tiering Criteria (Evaluate Across These 4 Dimensions)</h2>
<blockquote>
<p>Checklist: Before starting tiering, have your team answer these 4 questions</p>
</blockquote>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Dimension</th>
<th>Evaluation Content</th>
<th>Common Criteria</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Budget Scale</strong></td>
<td>Daily budget range, historical spend</td>
<td>Top-tier accounts typically exceed $100K/month</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Lifecycle Stage</strong></td>
<td>New, growth, stable, decline</td>
<td>New accounts have a test period, usually 0-30 days</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Monetization</strong></td>
<td>CPA, ROI, LTV performance</td>
<td>Benchmark-based pass/fail</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Maturity</strong></td>
<td>Creative inventory, account weight, scalability</td>
<td>Determines if increased investment is worthwhile</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Execution Steps:</strong><br />
1. Export core metrics for all accounts over the past 30 days<br />
2. Score each dimension separately (1-5 scale)<br />
3. Calculate weighted composite scores<br />
4. Divide into tiers: S (top), A (main), B (underperforming), C (test/phase-out)</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Define Operational Strategy for Each Tier</h2>
<p>Tiering is not the goal—ensuring each account receives appropriate operational actions is.</p>
<p><strong>S-Tier Accounts (Top Performers)</strong><br />
- Priority for testing new creatives and targeting<br />
- Stable budget with no sudden fluctuations<br />
- Assigned to senior advertisers for dedicated maintenance<br />
- Weekly deep-dive reviews focused on long-term ROI over daily CPA</p>
<p><strong>A-Tier Accounts (Core Accounts)</strong><br />
- Maintain stable delivery rhythm<br />
- Moderate frequency of creative A/B testing<br />
- Immediate adjustment when decline signals appear</p>
<p><strong>B-Tier Accounts (Underperforming)</strong><br />
- Investigate structural issues: overlapping ad groups, narrow targeting<br />
- Concentrate testing on 2-3 new creative sets<br />
- Set optimization deadline (e.g., downgraded if no improvement in 2 weeks)</p>
<p><strong>C-Tier Accounts (Test/Phase-out)</strong><br />
- New account testing: 1-month observation period<br />
- Persistently underperforming accounts: reduce budget or pause<br />
- Avoid resource waste, cut losses promptly</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Divide Team Responsibilities</h2>
<blockquote>
<p>Checklist: Every account has an assigned owner, no "unclaimed accounts"</p>
</blockquote>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Account Tier</th>
<th>Owner</th>
<th>Core Responsibilities</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>S-Tier</td>
<td>Senior advertiser/manager</td>
<td>Strategy formulation, budget adjustment, risk control</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>A-Tier</td>
<td>Mid-level advertiser</td>
<td>Daily optimization, creative testing</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>B-Tier</td>
<td>Mid-level/junior advertiser</td>
<td>Problem diagnosis, follow optimization</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>C-Tier</td>
<td>Junior advertiser/intern</td>
<td>Basic monitoring, data recording</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Recommended Rules:</strong><br />
- Each person manages no more than 2 S-tier accounts<br />
- Each person manages 5-8 A-tier accounts<br />
- Rotate account assignments periodically to avoid "comfort zones"</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Establish Account Re-tiering Mechanism</h2>
<p>Tiering is not a one-time task—it requires dynamic adjustment.</p>
<p><strong>Triggering Re-tiering:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Upgrade (S/A/B → Higher)</strong>: ROI exceeds baseline by 20% for 2 consecutive weeks</li>
<li><strong>Downgrade (S/A/B → Lower)</strong>: Spend decreases by 50% for 2 consecutive weeks without clear reason</li>
<li><strong>Direct Phase-out</strong>: Single account losses for 30+ consecutive days</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Execution Steps:</strong><br />
1. Auto-generate account tiering report every Monday<br />
2. Operations lead reviews re-tiering suggestions<br />
3. Weekly meeting confirms and notifies responsible parties<br />
4. Responsible party updates account tags and task assignments</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 5: Configure Differentiated Financial Controls</h2>
<p>Tiering directly impacts priority for funding, alerts, and financial reconciliation.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Checklist: Set different financial rules based on account tier</p>
</blockquote>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Financial Process</th>
<th>S-Tier</th>
<th>A-Tier</th>
<th>B/C-Tier</th>
